#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534105} :activity/id #uuid "c6e317c7-d19b-5509-8fc3-a09f2777d791" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048395 :topic/id #uuid "9bb55214-36f6-5124-91d2-24ff1cb653aa" :topic/math-academy-id 3242} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534105 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083834f0-9bf1-5b96-ab34-e2a726a19d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=7x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422961} {:answer/id #uuid "329126be-3296-51e6-af07-1508950c1b29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422966} {:answer/id #uuid "879273c7-bb2b-55dd-b5d2-dbaa262517cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422974} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8c52e8-48bd-5ead-ba09-dce5bd0183da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422975} {:answer/id #uuid "96647981-19ea-5f8c-a90f-017ed2cbe4a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422976}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422975} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e6571d0-2b85-5e20-ad0d-0458eb1f4ac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d20e4de9-7a35-5187-8874-829d308828ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-332721" :question/problem "![](images/15/1515f63552e99b9ca9e1780f9689ac5d148969aaa1dccfc2ba55bcb87e534b34.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1686fe8b-2f6a-5497-b24c-e3b125e58c6d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534124}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70d45c8d-cc13-5a1b-beb7-968053e2bd8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419973} {:answer/id #uuid "7728792e-46b5-5d38-8ba0-da19663ace00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419974}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419974} :answer-field/id #uuid "0213fba8-0aae-5642-a445-07bb59e08746" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420005} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f9f0a79-fb0f-5f4e-9fe8-53df6cb53d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419978} {:answer/id #uuid "d06ff763-ab36-521d-89ac-a4ece126dda9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419991}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419978} :answer-field/id #uuid "1601144d-8231-50b4-86fa-aadedbf6075d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420007} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4d54985-48c6-5b8f-be6d-43139558726a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419997} {:answer/id #uuid "edc9f0c6-e776-585d-b355-191e0404c0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420001} {:answer/id #uuid "fa87b52e-dd41-5530-8584-7afc34ae6f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419997} :answer-field/id #uuid "21385317-1cc7-529a-97be-528ef8b2772b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "637ffaff-c65f-5c9b-99c9-53a0db0e19a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-333211" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/ae/ae1135571904a4bfb49eb79e187fa6d1754fc562fe66b604d33eb555418a1345.png)"} :step/id #uuid "2dc79aa6-3794-566a-8863-d7c1360b7aa8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534134}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085486de-a0c1-555a-9181-b4b425a4fe03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ef/ef7ed85b18002aeec299826750ecc6306c90db8bf2c9bbd9bd8173af0ee9ea41.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305229} {:answer/id #uuid "6560fb87-3260-5d7b-9f4a-a880c5cbdb8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b9/b930f98928e3c4af06434c720d953ad0fcee17500113ed9a72492eda4219a7fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306127} {:answer/id #uuid "a66b10fb-fc03-5ace-b7f9-3b049191a810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0b/0b2cfbe650613293fe807be4ff7e38fa6f10b2e792dae8f7f4b78f27560f59d7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306718} {:answer/id #uuid "bc1a1504-015b-5c38-b69c-6616892e04ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e8/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306904} {:answer/id #uuid "c3faec70-b3c6-579b-bf25-6087a8f1f205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ff/ff9ffa100793101811d8aa6e0e95587ec912ef1e3f64d59fbac617a521479a5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306976}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306904} :answer-field/id #uuid "8230b11d-8bb8-5c4c-873b-8b24da7d6f03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bf46b344-09be-5328-8704-8fe72b540c14" :question/math-academy-id "q-333184" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{6t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/24/2473117cdca4318187b94374820c0a12576e998e7f631a6e7bf51e56c985be43.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{6t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{6{c}_{1}{e}^{6t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/e8/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png)"} :step/id #uuid "36b9a2bf-e189-57ad-8bb3-e1abb17b0ae7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534116}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391057 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007e1a63-77b7-5eff-8905-bffd2a2a8d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9f/9fb1f3c545855e7bdfe591574a9069e69bdcc0cc750e5fc4f5aa3eccfdc7ea5a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391059} {:answer/id #uuid "b3aa6c52-d997-5f86-9c55-1e2a11e1e45b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ec/eceeabfeb428687bb9f630fe3578937c3fd4fb910605dc25daf56ad5ce18a68a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391060} {:answer/id #uuid "014409ab-717d-51b8-a1b0-0a3eaabb3c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/64/64c0ac27236c4bfae452572e6b5b4c1d6184c45ebac9149ca4fc442d7533bb51.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391061} {:answer/id #uuid "ec30175e-4dad-5eac-9ff8-b58cc3249ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9f/9ff104bc583ec025ede6de68f7ea546dea99c914153b8e2325c6f2f9188d0a9b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391062} {:answer/id #uuid "bde5fb9a-33eb-5722-aa37-4cab11937c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/20/20ef7519d0ca517d0d7d06d2998f7bd6c77f04145243f721e197fbef5c087791.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391063}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391063} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b3e8fa4-883f-5cc4-809d-2815c7f4d579" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7a71e1e9-c69e-580f-aa68-2f882f086377" :question/math-academy-id "q-332751" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{7t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/96/96801605aa5d777eb18c13ded1f7b7e59ae74968b210da3b7a26f339a1c9f673.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{7t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{7t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{3t})^{′}}=\\frac{7{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}}{3{c}_{1}{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{7{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/20/20ef7519d0ca517d0d7d06d2998f7bd6c77f04145243f721e197fbef5c087791.png)"} :step/id #uuid "56a204a2-e03c-5e29-b487-c8e57e8693f7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534117}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534134 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308411 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417e2f25-d39f-59c2-845f-c9528b2ce07d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305797} {:answer/id #uuid "4de865c2-71c4-59ab-ba69-f06587c355a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305907} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac69e4a-4d92-599e-94d0-f37b8891b475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306324} {:answer/id #uuid "c12d5bf2-b9ad-5ec3-a445-0a58631379d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306954}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306954} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cba2e14-9855-5caa-b80d-4e097a8eac3b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307759} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0436876d-60a1-53e5-ba1b-6e7feab5be42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305189} {:answer/id #uuid "805fd504-b75d-5588-8806-e07e8cf4b9ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306376} {:answer/id #uuid "9c700a04-4e68-5484-87e4-1d9ec4c6785d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306612} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e7722e0-4e5a-5615-ae93-3155340ac93b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307879} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f938149-0198-5c73-a108-bf8eabdfcbd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305285} {:answer/id #uuid "5aed24e9-d896-5aad-9640-01713cd163b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306022} {:answer/id #uuid "d11e8324-8372-55e2-a2c7-1c109212b1d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307086}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305285} :answer-field/id #uuid "d571594f-2518-5623-8366-f5312bc34af9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2f2e900b-fa9b-5f5e-ba9a-c9406243da7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333218" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/65/657508496ddc73c386545152b45cc628d9abddd13ec069daf169d1dc61c1f7be.png)"} :step/id #uuid "7b8cd9ab-93c4-58bf-896c-f561774db70b"}] :activity/title "Phase Portraits for Decoupled Linear Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533128}]}
